# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Native modules and ABI — read this before changing package.json
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The SQLite driver is `better-sqlite3-multiple-ciphers` (SQLCipher). Plain `better-sqlite3`
# accepts `PRAGMA cipher` and `PRAGMA hexkey` without error and then writes a fully cleartext
# database, which would silently violate SPEC.md §8's encryption-at-rest requirement.
#
# That driver is NOT a Node-API addon. Its binary is compiled against one specific
# NODE_MODULE_VERSION, so it must match the runtime that loads it:
#
#   Electron 42.8.0   ABI 146   <- what the app runs on, and what postinstall builds for
#   Node 26.5.1       ABI 147   <- plain `node` CANNOT load the app's binary
#
# Consequences, all of them already handled:
#
#   1. `postinstall` runs @electron/rebuild after install. npm's own install script fetches
#      the Node-ABI prebuild (147), which fails to dlopen in Electron with
#      ERR_DLOPEN_FAILED / NODE_MODULE_VERSION. Do not remove that step.
#
#   2. `npm test` runs under `ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE=1 electron`, not `node`. The store's
#      security tests (SPEC.md §12) open the real driver, so they have to run on the ABI the
#      app ships with. Running them under plain `node` fails at dlopen.
#
#   3. ELECTRON IS PINNED TO ^42. better-sqlite3-multiple-ciphers@12.11.1 publishes Electron
#      prebuilds only through ABI 146 (Electron 42). Electron 43 is ABI 148 with no published
#      prebuild, so bumping would force a from-source build — see the blocker below.
#      @electron/rebuild downloads the prebuild today; it never invokes a compiler.
#
# BLOCKER — compiling any native module from source currently fails:
#
#   This repository's path contains an apostrophe ("AIM but it's signal"). node-gyp shells
#   out through /bin/sh and dies with:
#       /bin/sh: -c: line 0: unexpected EOF while looking for matching "'"
#   electron-builder refuses outright: "Invalid output directory: contains unsupported
#   shell-special characters". Both were verified; both go away when the same tree is built
#   from a path without an apostrophe. Spaces alone are survivable — the apostrophe is not.
#
#   Rename the directory before bumping Electron past 42 or before packaging a release.
#
# Deliberately NOT set here: `runtime=electron` / `target=<version>`. Those would force npm to
# build EVERY native module against Electron at install time, including ones that build tools
# load under plain Node. @electron/rebuild targets only the driver.

# Apple Silicon only (see electron-builder.yml > mac). No universal or x64 artifacts; the arch
# is pinned on the electron-rebuild invocation rather than globally.

fund=false
audit=false
